From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a film starts through the prepared resume

Kodi's start sync anchors the clock from the queues' start times and
leaves the engine to correct what it got wrong once sound runs. On
passthrough that correction is a gap or a drop: starts measured 8, 44 and
116 ms off, and anything over 13.5 ms is corrected audibly.

The first start of a file now goes through the preparation a resume uses:
audio from its first packet, the picture held at a target a lead-in later
until the audio is measured running in sync, then both released together,
so no correction follows. The first frame stays on screen while the
lead-in plays (0.6-1 s, usually the film's black, silent opening).

A resume reaches its audio by seeking back and flushing. A start does
neither: its audio packets are held from the stream's open, queued and
untouched (the audio player paused), and taken from the first once the
display has changed mode, the sink has re-locked and the renderer has
passed on the display reset. The video player, already decoding, joins the
renderer's hold through a new VIDEO_PREROLL_ARM and decodes on from its
first picture, dropping up to the target. With no seek, a source that is
slow to seek (an index read at the end of a file on a sleeping disk) costs
nothing, and MVC, whose flush re-creates the decoder, starts this way too.
The sink opens once, after the mode change.

The audio player states its passthrough from the codec at open, as a held
start must know it before any packet. The streams-ready announcement
(fullscreen, OnAVStarted) is made when the preroll releases, as the start
sync makes it. The renderer's hold no longer refuses a pending display
reset, which only the next flip clears and a held start never makes; the
start waits instead until the video thread has passed the reset on (a
hold taken before it is failed by it). The pause and the start take their
hold through one helper, TakeNewResumeHold. A start that does not settle
within 8 s of its first picture, is not eligible, or meets a seek releases
its audio to the ordinary start; a failed preparation recovers to where
the file's streams begin. Whether the file can seek is known only after
its streams open, so the open holds on codec and passthrough alone and the
start decides the rest. A file opened in a running player (the next episode) can keep the last
one's audio stream when the format matches; its start holds that stream
the same way.

A film whose subtitle is not yet translated far enough ahead starts the
ordinary way, waiting for the translation (the subtitle group's wait)
with the stream players paused: held here, the decoder would reset
after 5 s without a picture taken.

Eligibility is the resume's without what only its flush needs, on a plain
file of ten seconds or more with video, no FEL: TrueHD, DTS, AC-3 or E-AC-3
passthrough, or any codec decoded to PCM. PCM goes through the preparation
a decoded frame at a time, as a bitstream a packet at a time: the sink takes
what fits without waiting and the player keeps the rest, the preroll counts
the share written, and the engine's sync state is read for PCM too. The
350 ms receiver lead is TrueHD passthrough's alone.

The release does not move the clock. It used to re-anchor it on the
submitted end less the delay, which read 97 ms away on an AC-3 start (30
ms on TrueHD), and the engine's correction a second later stepped the
clock back and dropped two frames. The clock now stays where the engine
has just put the audio, and what is left of the offset is the audio rate
trim's to walk out; the estimate only sanity-checks the measurement. The
pause's release behaves the same.

A start accepts a target picture up to a second past the one it asked
for, where a pause allows 100 ms. The display reset discards whatever the
decoder had queued through the mode change, 13 to 15 pictures here, so
the first picture left can be past the target by more than that, and the
start fell back to a seek.

The resume prepares the same codecs as the start: AC-3, E-AC-3 and
decoded PCM join TrueHD and DTS. An AC-3 resume measured 162 ms off and
was taken out by inserted silence and a clock step. A decoded stream's
play press now takes the deferred resume where it can be prepared, so a
preparation armed in the pause is not cancelled by it.

Shared code: the start sync and the preparation live in CVideoPlayer, the
hold in CRenderManager and the stream players; there is no AML side to it.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
